 ### [Mark Invisible Cells](4648-mark-invisible-cells.md)
-A method to mark cells that are not covered by any camera with density -1, executed once before training. It complements occupancy creation by handling visibility rather than spatial occupancy.
+Marks voxels not visible to any training camera as -1. Chosen when visibility culling is required before training. Differs by focusing on visibility rather than density-based block detection.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-**Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels, dominated by the convolution operation. The convolution is highly parallelizable on GPU.
-**Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for the input density and intermediate convolution output. The output mask is smaller by a factor of 8 in volume, but stored as a dense boolean array of size $(side-1)^3$.
-**Performance:** Fast on GPU due to JAX's optimized convolution primitives. [S1, unofficial]
-**Strengths:** Simple to implement; effectively reduces memory usage for sparse scenes; compatible with differentiable rendering pipelines if gradients are needed (though the thresholding step is non-differentiable, the convolution is).
-**Limitations:** Loses fine-grained detail within 2x2x2 blocks; assumes a uniform grid; the threshold of 0 may be too sensitive for noisy density fields.
-**When to use:** When memory is constrained and the scene has large empty regions. When using a differentiable renderer that benefits from sparse occupancy masks.
-**When not to use:** When high-frequency details are critical and cannot be captured by 2x2x2 blocks. When the density field is very noisy and requires smoothing before thresholding.
-
+**Time Complexity:** $O(S^3)$ for thresholding and $O(S^3)$ for convolution, where $S$ is the side length. The convolution is the dominant operation.
+**Memory Complexity:** $O(S^3)$ for storing the density matrix, occupancy matrix, and convolution output.
+**Performance:** Efficient on GPU due to JAX convolution optimization. [S1, unofficial]
+**Strengths:** Simple, differentiable (if using soft thresholding, though this method uses hard thresholding), and effectively reduces noise by requiring full 2x2x2 occupancy.
+**Limitations:** Hard thresholding is non-differentiable. Boundary effects may require careful handling. [S1, unofficial]
+**When to use:** When a sparse representation of fully occupied blocks is needed for efficient rendering or training.
+**When not to use:** When partial occupancy or smooth density gradients are required.
